## Usage

```bash
go run . hello --name world
```

## Development

```bash
go test ./...
make build
```

## Releases

Create a signed release commit and lightweight tag from clean, synchronized
`main`:

```sh
scripts/release.sh 0.0.1-alpha.0
git push --atomic origin main v0.0.1-alpha.0
```

The release command builds all four GoReleaser archives twice from the Git
index, requires byte-for-byte reproducibility, and signs the canonical
release-manifest digest in the release commit. Prereleases keep changelog
entries under `Unreleased`; stable releases create a dated changelog section.

The tag workflow validates and constructs artifacts in a read-only job, then
transfers only the exact artifact ID to GitHub-owned attestation and
publication jobs. Public repositories receive GitHub artifact attestations.
The attestation job is skipped for private repositories whose plan does not
support attestations; signed-manifest and publication revalidation remain
enforced.

After creating the GitHub repository:

- configure `github-release` to allow only `v*` tags;
- protect `main` with one code-owner approval, stale-review dismissal,
  conversation resolution, linear history, and force-push/deletion blocking;
- protect `v*` tags against creation, update, and deletion except for the
  repository-administrator release path.

Private repositories require a GitHub plan that supports these server-side
branch, tag, and environment restrictions. The checked-in signed-release
checks still apply when those plan features are unavailable.

Do not create annotated or signed tag objects. Release tags must be
lightweight tags pointing to the signed release commit.
